#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:462
msgid "Computed and related fields"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:467
msgid ""
"Computed fields are a core concept in Odoo and are used to define fields "
"that are computed based on other fields. This is useful for fields that are "
"derived from other fields, like a sum of sub-records (adding up the price of "
"all the items in a sale order)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:471
#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/08_compute_onchange.rst:18
msgid ""
"**Reference**: the documentation related to this topic can be found "
"in :ref:`reference/fields/compute`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:474
msgid ""
"Data modules can define computed fields of any type, but are quite limited "
"compared to Python modules. Indeed, since data modules are meant to be "
"deployed on systems that do not allow arbitrary code to run, the Python code "
"that is allowed is very limited."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:479
msgid ""
"All Python code written for data modules is executed in a sandboxed "
"environment that limits the operations that can be performed. For example, "
"you cannot import libraries, you cannot access any OS files, and you cannot "
"even print to the console. Some utilities are provided, but this varies with "
"the type of sandboxed environment that is used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:484
msgid ""
"In the case of compute methods, the sandbox is very limited and only "
"provides the bare minimum of utilities to allow the execution of the code. "
"In addition to the Python builtins, you also have access to the `datetime`, "
"`dateutil` and `time` modules (e.g., to help with date calculations)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:489
msgid ""
"Note also that \"dot assignation\" is disabled in the sandbox, so you cannot "
"write ``property.x_total_area = 1`` in the compute method. You have to use "
"dictionary access: ``property['x_total_area'] = 1``. Dot notation for field "
"*access* works normally: ``property.x_garden_area`` will return the value of "
"the ``x_garden_area`` field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:495
msgid ""
"We previously defined two \"area\" fields on our ``x_estate.property`` "
"model: ``living_area`` and ``garden_area``. To define a computed field on "
"the model that returns the sum of the two areas, we can add the following "
"code to our data module:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:518
msgid ""
"Whilst in server actions, you iterate on a `records` variable, in the case "
"of a computed field, you iterate on a `self` variable that contains the "
"recordset on which the field is computed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:521
msgid ""
"The ``depends`` attribute is used to define the fields that the computed "
"field depends on and the ``compute`` attribute is used to define the code "
"that is executed to compute the field (using Python code)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:525
msgid ""
"Unlike in Python modules, computed fields are stored by default. If you wish "
"for a computed field to not be stored (e.g., for performance reasons or to "
"avoid database bloat), you can set the ``store`` attribute to ``False``. The "
"same applies to ``readonly``: if you want your computed field to not be "
"editable, you need to set the ``readonly`` attribute to ``True``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:530
msgid ""
"The `CDATA` section is used to specify to XML parsers that the content is a "
"string and not XML; this prevents the parser from trying to interpret the "
"Python code as XML, or the addition of extra space, etc. when the code gets "
"inserted into the database at module install time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:536
msgid ""
"Add a computed field to the ``x_estate.property`` model that returns the sum "
"of the ``x_living_area`` and ``x_garden_area`` fields, as shown above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:538
msgid "Include the field in the form view of the ``x_estate.property`` model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:541
msgid ""
"Unlike in Python modules, it is not possible to define an inverse or search "
"method for computed fields."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:547
msgid ""
"Related fields are a simplified form of computed fields that mirror the "
"value of another field through a many2one relationship."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:550
msgid ""
"**Reference**: the documentation related to this topic can be found "
"in :ref:`reference/fields/related`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:553
msgid ""
"Related fields can be of any type (the type of the field at the other end of "
"the relation traversal). They are defined as if one were adding the field "
"directly to the model with the addition of a ``related`` attribute that "
"specifies the target field on the related model that contains the value to "
"be mirrored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:558
msgid ""
"For example, if we want to access the country of the buyer directly from the "
"``x_estate.property`` model, we can add the following code to our data "
"module:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:575
msgid ""
"The ``related`` attribute is used to specify the target field on the related "
"model that contains the value to be mirrored. This must be a dot-separated "
"list of field names."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:579
msgid "Code and business logic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:582
msgid "Server actions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:584
msgid ""
"In a Python module, you are free to define any method on your model. One "
"common usage pattern is to add so-called \"actions\" methods to your model "
"then bind these methods to buttons in the UI (e.g to confirm a quote, post "
"an invoice, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:588
msgid ""
"In a data module, you can achieve the same effect by defining :ref:`Server "
"Actions <reference/actions/server>` bound to your model. Server actions "
"represent pieces of logic that are run dynamically on the server. These "
"actions can be configured manually in the database directly via "
"the :menuselection:`Settings --> Technical --> Actions --> Server Actions` "
"menu and can be of different types; in our case, we will use the ``code`` "
"type which allows us to run any Python code in a sandboxed environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:596
msgid ""
"This environment contains several utilities to help you interact with the "
"Odoo database:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:598
msgid "``env``: the environment of the record"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:599
msgid "``model``: the model of the record"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:600
msgid "``user`` and ``uid``: the current user and their id"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:601
msgid ""
"``datetime``, ``dateutil``, ``timezone`` and ``time``: libraries to help "
"with date/time calculations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:603
msgid ""
"``float_compare``: a utility function to compare two float values with a "
"given precision"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:604
msgid ""
"``b64encode`` and ``b64decode``: utility functions to encode and decode "
"values in base64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:605
msgid ""
"``Command``: a utility class to help build complex expressions and commands "
"(see the `Command` class in the :ref:`ORM reference <reference/fields/"
"relational>`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:608
msgid ""
"In addition, you have access to the recordset on which the action is "
"executed (typically a single record when the action is executed from a form "
"view, and multiple records when the action is executed from a list view) via "
"the ``record`` and ``records`` variables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:613
msgid ""
"If your action needs to return an action to the client (for example to "
"redirect the user to another view), you can assign it to a an ``action`` "
"variable inside your server action's code. The code sandbox will inspect the "
"variables defined in your code after its execution and will automatically "
"return it if it detects the presence of an ``action`` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:618
msgid ""
"If the `website` module is installed, the `request` object will be available "
"in the code sandbox and you can assign a `response` object to the `response` "
"variable to return a response to the client in a similar way. This is "
"explored in more details in the :ref:`tutorials/importable_modules/"
"website_controllers` section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:623
msgid ""
"For example, we could define an action on the ``x_estate.property`` model "
"that sets the ``x_status`` field of all its offers to ``Refused``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:638
msgid ""
"To include this action as a button in the form view of the "
"``x_estate.property`` model, we can add the following :ref:`button "
"<reference/view_architectures/form/button>` node in the header of our form "
"view:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:649
msgid ""
"It is also possible to add an entry in the gear icon (:icon:`fa-gear`) to "
"run this action (e.g. to avoid adding buttons to views that are already "
"crowded). To do so, you can *bind* your server action to the model and to "
"specific types of views:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:667
msgid ""
"This will make the action available in the gear icon (:icon:`fa-gear`) of "
"the ``x_estate.property`` model, in the list (when one or more records are "
"selected via the checkbox) and form views."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:672
msgid ""
"Add a server action to the ``x_estate.property.offer`` model that sets the "
"``x_status`` field of an offer to ``Accepted`` and updates the selling price "
"and buyer of the property to which the offer is attached accordingly. This "
"action should also mark all the other offers on the same property as "
"``Refused``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:676
msgid ""
"Include a button in the embedded list view of offers that allows to execute "
"this action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:682
msgid "Overriding Python models"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:685
msgid "Via UI elements"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:687
msgid ""
"Unlike in Python modules, it is not possible to override a Python model's "
"method cleanly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:689
msgid ""
"However, it is possible (in some cases) to replace the elements of the UI "
"that call these methods and to intercept the calls to these methods in a "
"server action."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:692
msgid ""
"A typical example would be an integration with the ``Sales`` app of Odoo. "
"Let's imagine that your Real Estate module integrates with the Sales "
"application so that when a specific product is sold (e.g., a quote for "
"managing the sale of a property), you want to automatically create a new "
"property record in your module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:697
msgid "To achieve this, you will need to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:699
msgid ""
"create a server action that calls the original method of the button and add "
"custom logic before or after that method call"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:701
msgid ""
"replace the button in the view with a custom button that calls the server "
"action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:741
msgid "Via automation rules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:743
msgid ""
"Automations rules are a way to automatically execute actions on records in "
"the database based on specific triggers, like state changes, addition of a "
"tag, etc. They can be useful to tie behaviour to life-cycle events of "
"records, for example by sending an email when an offer is accepted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:747
msgid ""
"Using automation rules for extending a standard behaviour can be more robust "
"than the UI-based approach since it will also run if the life-cycle event is "
"triggered in another way than via a button (e.g., via a webhook or a direct "
"call to the method; for example when a quote is confirmed via the portal or "
"the e-commerce). They are however a bit more finicky to set up properly, as "
"one needs to ensure that the automation will only run at the proper moment "
"by setting up specific fields to watch, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:754
msgid ""
"**Documentation**: a more complete documentation related to this topic can "
"be found in :doc:`/applications/studio/automated_actions`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:758
msgid ""
"Automation Rules are not part of the ``base`` module; they come with the "
"``base_automation`` module; so if you define automation rules in your data "
"module, you need to make sure that ``base_automation`` is part of your "
"module's dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:762
msgid ""
"Once installed, Automation Rules are managed via "
"the :menuselection:`Settings --> Technical --> Automations --> Automation "
"Rules` menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:765
msgid ""
"Automation Rules are particularly useful to tie a data module to an existing "
"standard Odoo module. Since data modules cannot override methods, tying "
"automation to life-cycle changes of standard models is a common way to "
"extend standard modules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:769
msgid ""
"If we were to rewrite our example from the previous section using automation "
"rules, a few changes would be needed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:772
msgid ""
"the server action should no longer call the original method of the button "
"(instead, the original method will trigger the change that will fire the "
"automation rule)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:774
msgid "the view extension is not needed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:775
msgid ""
"we need to define an Automation Rule to trigger the server action on the "
"appropriate event"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:805
msgid ""
"Note that the :ref:`XML IDs <tutorials/define_module_data/xml_id>` to "
"standard Odoo models, fields, selection values, etc. can be found in the "
"Odoo instance itself by navigating to the appropriate record in the "
"technical menus and using the ``View Metadata`` menu entry of the debug "
"menu. XML IDs for models are simply the model name with dots replaced by "
"underscores and prefixed by ``model_`` (e.g., ``sale.model_sale_order`` is "
"``sale.order`` as defined in the `sale` module); XML IDs for fields are the "
"model name with dots replaced by underscores and prefixed by ``field_``, the "
"model's name and the field name (e.g., ``sale.field_sale_order__name`` is "
"the XML ID for the ``name`` field of the ``sale.order`` model which is "
"defined in the `sale` module)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:817
msgid "Website controllers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:819
msgid ""
"HTTP Controllers in Odoo are usually defined in the :file:`controllers` "
"directory of a module. In data modules, it is possible to define server "
"actions that behave as controllers if the website module is installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:823
msgid ""
"When the website module is installed, server actions can be marked as "
"`Available on the website` and given a path (the full path is always "
"prefixed with `/website/action` to avoid URL collisions); the global "
"`request` object is made available in the local scope of the code server "
"action."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:827
msgid ""
"The `request` object provides several methods to access the body of the "
"request:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:829
msgid ""
"`request.get_http_params()`: extract key-value pairs from the query string "
"and the forms present in the body (both `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` "
"and `multipart/form-data`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:831
msgid ""
"`request.get_json_data()`: extract the JSON data from the body of the "
"request."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:833
msgid ""
"Since it is not possible to return a value from within a server action, to "
"define the response to return, one can assign a response-like object to the "
"`response` variable, which will be returned to the website automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:837
msgid ""
"Here is an example of a simple website controller that will return a list of "
"properties when the URL `/website/action/estate` is called:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:857
msgid ""
"Several useful methods are available in the `request` object to facilitate "
"the generation of the response object:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:860
msgid ""
"`request.render(template, qcontext=None, lazy=True, **kw)` to render a QWeb "
"template using its xmlid; the extra keyword arguments are forwarded to the "
"`werkzeug.Response` object (e.g. to set cookies, headers, etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:863
msgid ""
"`request.redirect(location, code=303, local=True)` to redirect to a "
"different URL; the `local` argument is used to specify whether the "
"redirection should be relative to the website or not (default: `True`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:866
msgid ""
"`request.not_found()` to return a `werkzeug.HTTPException` exception to "
"signal a 404 error to the website."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:868
msgid ""
"`request.make_response(data, headers=None, cookies=None, status=200)` to "
"manually create a `werkzeug.Response` object; the `status` argument is the "
"HTTP status code to return (default: 200)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:871
msgid ""
"`request.make_json_response(data, headers=None, cookies=None, status=200)` "
"to manually create a JSON response; the data will be json-serialized using "
"`json.dumps` utility; this can be useful to set up server-to-server "
"communications via API calls."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:875
msgid ""
"For implementation details or other (less common) methods, refer to the "
"`Request` object's implementation in the `odoo.http` module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:878
msgid ""
"Note that security concerns are left to the developer (typically through "
"security rules or by using `sudo` to access records)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/importable_modules.rst:882
msgid ""
"The model used in the `model_id` field of the server action must be "
"accessible to the public user for the write operation for this server action "
"to run; otherwise the server action will return a 403 error. A way to avoid "
"giving access is to link your server action to a model that is already "
