#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:3
msgid "Chapter 4: Security - A Brief Introduction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:5
msgid ""
"In the :doc:`previous chapter <03_basicmodel>`, we created our first table "
"intended to store business data. In a business application such as Odoo, one "
"of the first questions to consider is who\\ [#who]_ can access the data. "
"Odoo provides a security mechanism to allow access to the data for specific "
"groups of users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:10
msgid ""
"The topic of security is covered in more detail in :doc:`../"
"restrict_data_access`. This chapter aims to cover the minimum required for "
"our new module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:14
msgid "Data Files (CSV)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:16
msgid ""
"Odoo is a highly data driven system. Although behavior is customized using "
"Python code, part of a module's value is in the data it sets up when loaded. "
"One way to load data is through a CSV file. One example is the `list of "
"country states <https://github.com/odoo/odoo/blob/19.0/odoo/addons/base/data/"
"res.country.state.csv>`_ which is loaded at installation of the `base` "
"module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:31
#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/05_firstui.rst:81
msgid ""
"``id`` is an :term:`external identifier`. It can be used to refer to the "
"record (without knowing its in-database identifier)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:33
msgid ""
"``country_id:id`` refers to the country by using its :term:`external "
"identifier`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:34
msgid "``name`` is the name of the state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:35
msgid "``code`` is the code of the state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:37
msgid ""
"These three fields are `defined <https://github.com/odoo/odoo/blob/"
"2ad2f3d6567b6266fc42c6d2999d11f3066b282c/odoo/addons/base/models/"
"res_country.py#L108-L111>`__ in the ``res.country.state`` model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:41
msgid ""
"By convention, a file importing data is located in the ``data`` folder of a "
"module. When the data is related to security, it is located in the "
"``security`` folder. When the data is related to views and actions (we will "
"cover this later), it is located in the ``views`` folder. Additionally, all "
"of these files must be declared in the ``data`` list within the "
"``__manifest__.py`` file. Our example file is defined `in the manifest of "
"the base module <https://github.com/odoo/odoo/blob/"
"e8697f609372cd61b045c4ee2c7f0fcfb496f58a/odoo/addons/base/"
"__manifest__.py#L29>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:48
msgid ""
"Also note that the content of the data files is only loaded when a module is "
"installed or updated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:53
msgid ""
"The data files are sequentially loaded following their order in the "
"``__manifest__.py`` file. This means that if data ``A`` refers to data "
"``B``, you must make sure that ``B`` is loaded before ``A``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:57
msgid ""
"In the case of the country states, you will note that the `list of countries "
"<https://github.com/odoo/odoo/blob/e8697f609372cd61b045c4ee2c7f0fcfb496f58a/"
"odoo/addons/base/__manifest__.py#L22>`__ is loaded **before** the `list of "
"country states <https://github.com/odoo/odoo/blob/"
"e8697f609372cd61b045c4ee2c7f0fcfb496f58a/odoo/addons/base/"
"__manifest__.py#L29>`__. This is because the states refer to the countries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:63
msgid ""
"Why is all this important for security? Because all the security "
"configuration of a model is loaded through data files, as we'll see in the "
"next section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:69
msgid ""
"**Reference**: the documentation related to this topic can be found "
"in :ref:`reference/security/acl`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:74
msgid ""
"**Goal**: at the end of this section, the following warning should not "
"appear anymore:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:80
msgid ""
"When no access rights are defined on a model, Odoo determines that no users "
"can access the data. It is even notified in the log:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:88
msgid ""
"Access rights are defined as records of the model ``ir.model.access``. Each "
"access right is associated with a model, a group (or no group for global "
"access) and a set of permissions: create, read, write and unlink\\ "
"[#unlink]_. Such access rights are usually defined in a CSV file named "
"``ir.model.access.csv``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:94
msgid "Here is an example for our previous `test_model`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/developer/tutorials/server_framework_101/04_securityintro.rst:101
